-- `l4 render` leaves out a definition that is only test data: a constant that
-- an #EVAL or #ASSERT names and no rule uses. A rule with no GIVEN of its own
-- whose body is a call has the shape of such a constant, and until 2026-11-01
-- that was enough: the rule vanished from the document when a directive named
-- it, with no message. Now a definition whose value depends on an input is
-- never test data.
--
-- A rule under a section GIVEN has no GIVEN of its own and reads the
-- section's inputs, so every such rule that calls another one was exposed.
-- `may vote` was rendered on unstable 8e19faa0d and dropped once the port of
-- legalese/l4-ide#802 gave the own-line @nlg to the rule below it.

§ `Eligibility`
    GIVEN age IS A NUMBER
@nlg the person is an adult
`is adult` MEANS age AT LEAST 18

@nlg the person may vote
`may vote` MEANS `is adult`

`may stand` MEANS `is adult`

§ `Outside a section GIVEN`

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`twice` n MEANS n TIMES 2

-- The controls: a call whose arguments are all constants, and a bare literal,
-- depend on no input. Only a directive names them, so they are test data, and
-- are still left out.
`the doubled fee` MEANS `twice` 21

`the test age` MEANS 20

#EVAL `may vote` WITH age IS `the test age`
#EVAL `may stand` WITH age IS 20
#EVAL `the doubled fee`
